    "https://careers.cccu.org/career-resources/find-the-job-1/adjunct-faculty-jobs-at-christian-colleges-roles-pay-and-day-to-day-2026-108": {
      "title": "The federal occupation an adjunct teaching line sits inside",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 | Council for Christian Colleges and Universities Home Search Directory Contact Us Career Resources Career Advice Interview Coach Offer Analyzer Pricing Register Job Seekers Employers Sign In Post a Job Career Advice Find the Job Adjunct Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 Adjunct Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 An adjunct appointment at a Christian college is a contract to teach a named course in a named term, and most of what a candidate wants to know about it is settled by the posting and the institution's own policies rather than by anything national. The federal wage tables still tell you something, provided you know which occupation the appointment sits inside. The Bureau of Labor Statistics put the median annual wage for Postsecondary Teachers, All Other (SOC 25-1199) at $72,400 inside privately owned colleges, universities and professional schools in May 2025. That is the nearest published federal figure to a teaching line at a private institution, and it is not a per-course rate. For Employers Hire faculty and staff for your campus Post your opening where faculty, student-life and campus-ministry professionals search first. Post a Job → Job Seekers Find your next role at a Christian college Browse faculty, administration and student-life jobs at faith-based colleges and universities. Browse Jobs → Key takeaway: A part-time teaching appointment falls inside SOC 25-1199, Postsecondary Teachers, All Other, or inside the code for the discipline where BLS breaks that discipline out. No federal series isolates faith-based institutions, and BLS publishes no hourly or per-course wage for these teaching codes at all. The CCCU's hiring standard reaches full-time faculty members and administrators only, so whatever an adjunct appointment requires on the faith side is set and published by the individual institution. The federal occupation an adjunct teaching line sits inside There is no occupation in the federal statistical system called adjunct professor, and none called Christian college faculty. Nothing in the Standard Occupational Classification, in O*NET or in the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics program isolates faculty at a faith-based institution. That is how the classification is built, and every number below has to be read through it. The code is 25-1199, and it is a residual bucket BLS defines 25-1199, Postsecondary Teachers, All Other as covering \"All postsecondary teachers not listed separately.\" It is what remains after every field BLS reports separately has been removed, so it is not a general college professor average. Because it is an \"All Other\" residual title, O*NET publishes nothing for it - no tasks, no work context, no education distribution. One correction before it costs you a search: the code is 25-1199, not 25-1099. There is no 25-1099 in the 2018 Standard Occupational Classification, and the O*NET crosswalk search for it returns no matches. What the wage tables publish, and what they do not Across all industries, OEWS counted 149,840 jobs in 25-1199 in May 2025 at an annual median of $77,640. Inside privately owned colleges, universities and professional schools - NAICS 611305, every private college in the country, secular institutions included - the same code shows 51,570 jobs at an annual mean of $84,950 and an annual median of $72,400 in May 2025. That is a private-college number, not a Christian-college number, and BLS publishes industry and public-versus-private splits nationally only. The OEWS frequently-asked-questions page answers it directly: \"No. BLS publishes estimates by industry and public/private ownership type at the national level only.\" So the sentence salary articles reach for - what Christian college faculty earn in a given state - cannot be built at all. The spread matters more than the midpoint. Annual percentiles for 25-1199 in May 2025 run $44,300 at the 10th, $59,290 at the 25th, $77,640 at the median, $106,770 at the 75th and $160,700 at the 90th - a 3.6-fold range inside one occupation, because the code pools fields with nothing in common except that BLS does not report them separately. Then the limit that matters most here: BLS publishes no hourly wage for 25-1199 at all , not a mean and not a single percentile. Every hourly cell carries footnote 4, which reads that wages for some occupations that do not generally work year-round, full time, are reported either as hourly wages or annual salaries depending on how they are typically paid. The same holds for 25-1126, 25-1081, 25-1122 and 25-1123. There is no published federal per-course rate, and dividing an annual figure by 2,080 produces a number nobody measured. The published cuts are national, national-by-industry and, for a few states, a state total; none is for part-time appointments and none is for faith-based institutions. Growth and openings come from a different program Wages come from OEWS, an establishment survey of wage-and-salary jobs; employment, growth and openings come from the Employment Projections matrix, which is economy-wide and includes the self-employed, so the two never agree. For 25-1199 the projections put employment at 183,400 in 2024 rising to 186,800 in 2034, up 1.8 percent, with about 13,500 openings a year, against 3.1 percent for all occupations. The cheerier figure you may have seen belongs elsewhere: the Occupational Outlook Handbook profile for Postsecondary Teachers projects 7 percent growth for 2024-34, but its own table lists 35 detailed codes running 25-1011 through 25-1193, and 25-1199 is not among them. Teach a discipline BLS reports separately and the code changes All figures below are OEWS May 2025 annual medians, cross-industry unless stated. Philosophy and Religion Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1126): 20,460 jobs at $80,260; inside privately owned colleges and universities nationally, 13,750 jobs at $78,660. The code covers philosophy faculty as well as religion and theology faculty, at every institution type. English Language and Literature Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1123): 57,720 jobs at $78,760 - the code behind the composition sections that fill a general-education core, treated at length in the piece on composition and literature teaching lines at Christian colleges . Communications Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1122): 29,420 jobs at $78,580. Education Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1081): 60,830 jobs at $75,350 - college-level teacher preparation, not K-12 classroom teaching. Nursing Instructors and Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1072): 77,960 jobs at $80,250. Nursing faculty are 25-1072, not 25-1071 Health Specialties Teachers, Postsecondary, whose 2018 SOC definition excludes them by name - a distinction unpacked in the article on nursing and health sciences teaching appointments at faith-based institutions . Each covers its occupation at every institution type nationally, and none is a Christian-college figure - a limit worked through in the guide to what the federal data can and cannot tell you about Christian college pay . The academic year, in the order the work happens Before the term: the assignment and the credential file An adjunct hire usually begins with a course that needs covering and a chair who has to justify in writing that the person covering it is qualified. That justification is an accreditation obligation on the institution, not a licence you carry. Accreditation comes from an agency recognized by the U.S. Department of Education; 34 CFR 602.3 defines an \"Institutional accrediting agency\" as \"an agency that accredits institutions of higher education\" and a \"Programmatic accrediting agency\" as \"an agency that accredits specific educational programs.\" There is no federal category called a regional accreditor: that distinction was eliminated by the Department's 2019 rule at 84 FR 58834, effective 1 July 2020, which states that \"the Department applies the same standards for recognition to both national and regional accrediting agencies.\" What goes into the file varies by accreditor. The Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC), whose standards bind only the institutions it accredits, requires that for each of its educational programs the institution justifies and documents the qualifications of its faculty members. Its own instructions add that \"The guidelines represent commonly-accepted good practice for the academic qualifications of faculty; however, SACSCOC recognizes that qualifications other than academic credentials (or combined with credentials) may be appropriate for teaching particular courses.\" That second sentence is the door practitioners walk through, and it does not override what an institution requires in a specific posting. Assemble transcripts, not a résumé: the chair needs graduate coursework in the teaching discipline in a form that survives a review. That file, more than the interview, is the real shape of a second-career move into Christian higher education . During the term: what the published task data shows Because O*NET publishes no descriptors for 25-1199, the nearest published task list belongs to 25-1126 Philosophy and Religion Teachers, Postsecondary. In O*NET database release 30.3 (May 2026) that occupation carries 22 tasks, and the highest-rated is \"Evaluate and grade students' class work, assignments, and papers,\" at an importance of 92, ahead of moderating classroom discussions at 91 and delivering lectures at 90. Those are importance ratings on a 0-100 scale, not percentages and not shares of workers. Read the order rather than the values: grading outranks lecturing, it is invisible in the course schedule, and it scales with enrollment rather than contact hours, so a three-credit section meeting three hours a week is not a three-hour-a-week commitment. The same occupation's work-context data records 69 percent of respondents reporting a typical work week of more than 40 hours against 22 percent reporting less than 40. Those rows show only the reported categories, do not sum to 100, and do not separate part-time appointments from full-time ones, so the 69 percent is not the hours behind one course. The end of the term is administrative: grades filed by the registrar's deadline. Whether there is a next assignment is a separate decision on a separate calendar, driven by section enrollment and the department's staffing plan. Pay rules that apply to teaching, and what binds whom Federal wage-and-hour regulation treats teaching differently from most other work. Under 29 CFR 541.303, a teaching professional is an employee \"with a primary duty of teaching, tutoring, instructing or lecturing ... as a teacher in an educational establishment,\" and the regulation provides that \"The requirements of 541.300 and Subpart G (salary requirements) of this part do not apply to the teaching professionals described in this section.\" An \"educational establishment,\" under 29 CFR 541.204(b), \"means an elementary or secondary school system, an institution of higher education or other educational institution.\" Two boundaries travel with that. It turns on a primary duty of teaching, so it does not reach non-teaching staff: admissions counselors, residence directors and advancement officers are tested under different rules that do include a salary threshold. And it is a classification, not a statement about what an appointment pays; questions about your own classification go to the institution's human-resources office. Keep the categories of obligation apart when reading a posting. Statutes and regulations bind everyone they cover. Accreditation standards are the private standards of a voluntary peer-review body that federal recognition gives consequences to. CCCU membership requirements, and the Evangelical Council for Financial Accountability standards the association asks members to keep their finances consistent with, bind only institutions that choose to join. The mission dimension, and how far the association's standard reaches The Council for Christian Colleges & Universities, a national association for Christian higher education since 1976, publishes a hiring requirement for its members. The wording in the Updated 2024-2025 CCCU Membership Application, core standard 5, is: CCCU voting members have a continuing institutional policy and practice, effective throughout membership, to hire as full-time faculty members and administrators only persons who profess faith in Jesus Christ. The live membership page at cccu.org, retrieved 2026-08-09, states the same requirement for governing and associate members. The scope is the whole point: it binds governing and associate members, not collaborative partners or international affiliates, and it reaches full-time faculty members and administrators. It does not reach adjunct or other part-time appointments, and it does not reach support staff. Nor does it say the opposite - that adjuncts face no requirement. It is silent about them, which leaves the question to each institution's own published policy, and some apply the same expectation to everyone who teaches. The association is not an accrediting body and holds no authority over an individual hire. The CCCU also publishes core standard 6, Christian Distinctions and Advocacy, under which governing and associate members voluntarily agree to align as institutions with the association's advocacy positions, decided by its Board of Directors and, the document says, changing over time. Its framework names four stated Christian beliefs, among them a position that human beings are created male and female in the image of God with intimate sexual relations intended for persons in a marriage between one man and one woman, together with the right of Christian institutions to maintain practices aligned with that ethic. It is a published criterion of a voluntary association, and it is material to anyone deciding where to apply. Three documents, not one, and they differ campus to campus A statement of faith, a doctrinal agreement and a lifestyle or community covenant are three different documents with different footing, and merging them into one phrase is how candidates get surprised at the signature stage. The CCCU's directory records 37 distinct denominational affiliations among its U.S. members; the most common single value is Nondenominational at 16 institutions, then Baptist at 13, Inter-denominational at 10, Southern Baptist at 8 and Christian Churches and Churches of Christ at 8. What one campus asks an adjunct to affirm is not what the next asks - see the piece on what the doctrinal documents in a Christian college application actually say . One structural rule explains many of the part-time lines in Bible and worldview courses. To be a CCCU member campus, at least 25 percent of the credits required for a baccalaureate degree must be general education, including at least 6 required credits in Biblical studies, Christian worldview or Christian ethics, of which at least 3 must be in Biblical studies. A member campus with no seminary still has that core to staff every term, and must also offer at least 15 majors - which is why a small institution runs a wide, thin curriculum and covers the thin parts part-time. How the same appointment differs by institution type NCES recorded 3,896 degree-granting postsecondary institutions in the United States in 2022-23, of which 1,536 were private nonprofit four-year institutions - the outer boundary of this employer market. Separately, as the association's own record rather than a federal count, the CCCU's live member directory listed 177 institutions on 2026-08-09, of which 131 carry a U.S. state address across 32 states. The small residential college and the larger private university At an institution of a thousand or so students the department may be three people, the search runs through a committee rather than a recruiting function, and the chair who calls you is also your only orientation. The general-education core, including the required Bible or worldview credits, is where most part-time demand sits, because those sections run every term regardless of major enrollments. At a larger private university the appointment is likelier to route through a faculty-affairs office with a standing adjunct pool - a longer paper trail, and usually a slower one. The seminary, the divinity school and the Bible college Graduate theological education runs on a separate accreditation layer. The ATS Commission on Accrediting - related to but distinct from The Association of Theological Schools - accredits graduate schools of theology, and its Standards of Accreditation, approved in June 2020, state at Standard 8.3 that faculty qualifications are typically demonstrated through each faculty member having an appropriate doctorate together with relevant professional, ecclesial or denominational experience, while allowing faculty without a doctorate where the school documents suitable qualifications. Those are the expectations a file is built against at a seminary or divinity school teaching appointment . Bible colleges sit on a third layer: accreditation in the Association for Biblical Higher Education is awarded by ABHE's Commission on Accreditation, not by ABHE itself. The layers never replace one another - a college can hold institutional accreditation while its theological school holds ATS Commission accreditation and a biblical-studies unit holds ABHE Commission accreditation . What no institution type asks for There is no credential for teaching at a Christian college: no Christian higher education certification, no CCCU certification, no Christian-college teaching credential, no faith-integration certificate. CCCU membership is held by an institution, never by a person. State teaching licensure does not apply either - that is the K-12 route. What is real is short: academic degrees; accreditation, which your employer holds and you do not; documented professional qualification where the accreditor's standard is justification rather than a fixed degree; and whatever doctrinal agreement the institution itself requires. Routes in for people whose graduate work is in something other than theology are set out in the article on the realistic routes into Christian college work without a theology degree . What the law says, quoted and left there Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 does not apply \"to a religious corporation, association, educational institution, or society with respect to the employment of individuals of a particular religion to perform work connected with the carrying on by such corporation, association, educational institution, or society of its activities\" (42 U.S.C. 2000e-1(a)). It further provides that it is not an unlawful employment practice \"for a school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning to hire and employ employees of a particular religion if such school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning is, in whole or in substantial part, owned, supported, controlled, or managed by a particular religion or by a particular religious corporation, association, or society, or if the curriculum of such school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning is directed toward the propagation of a particular religion\" (42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(e)(2)). Both provisions are limited to individuals of a particular religion. The EEOC's guidance states that religious organizations \"are subject to the Title VII prohibitions against discrimination on the basis of race, color, sex, national origin (as well as the anti-discrimination provisions of the ADEA, ADA, and GINA), and related retaliation, but are permitted to assert the statutory exemption as an affirmative defense.\" That guidance is the enforcing agency's stated position and not law; the EEOC Compliance Manual states on its face that its contents do not have the force and effect of law and are not meant to bind the public in any way. On the education-program side, Title IX provides that \"this section shall not apply to an educational institution which is controlled by a religious organization if the application of this subsection would not be consistent with the religious tenets of such organization\" (20 U.S.C. 1681(a)(3)), and 34 CFR 106.12(a) states that \"This part does not apply to an educational institution which is controlled by a religious organization to the extent application of this part would not be consistent with the religious tenets of such organization.\" Paragraph (b) adds that an institution \"is not required to seek assurance from the Assistant Secretary in order to assert such an exemption,\" so absence from any published list establishes nothing. The ministerial exception comes from Hosanna-Tabor Evangelical Lutheran Church and School v. EEOC, 565 U.S. 171 (2012), and Our Lady of Guadalupe School v. Morrissey-Berru, 591 U.S. ___ (2020), Nos. 19-267 and 19-348, decided July 8, 2020; both concerned K-12 schools, and whether it reaches faculty roles at a Christian college is contested and fact-specific. This is general information rather than legal advice - application depends on the facts, and the EEOC and qualified counsel are where that question goes. CCCU Career Center Hiring or job-hunting in Christian higher education? Post a Job → Browse Jobs → Frequently Asked Questions Does the CCCU hiring standard apply to adjunct faculty? Core standard 5 reaches full-time faculty members and administrators, and it binds governing and associate members. It does not extend to adjunct or other part-time appointments, to support staff, to collaborative partners or to international affiliates. That does not mean an adjunct appointment carries no faith requirement - it means the requirement, if there is one, is set and published by the individual institution rather than by the association. What does an adjunct course actually pay at a Christian college? No federal source answers that. The Bureau of Labor Statistics publishes annual wages for the teaching occupations, including a May 2025 annual median of $72,400 for Postsecondary Teachers, All Other (SOC 25-1199) inside privately owned colleges and universities nationally, but no hourly wage for that code and no per-course rate of any kind. There is also no federal cut for faith-based institutions and none for part-time appointments. The posting, the appointment letter and the department chair are the only sources that answer it for a specific course. Do I need a doctorate to teach as an adjunct? BLS records the typical entry-level education for Postsecondary Teachers, All Other (SOC 25-1199) as a doctoral or professional degree, which describes the occupation rather than any single posting. Accreditation standards generally require the institution to justify and document its faculty qualifications rather than apply one fixed degree rule, and SACSCOC states that qualifications other than academic credentials, or combined with credentials, may be appropriate for teaching particular courses. What a given course requires is whatever that institution documents. Is there a certification for teaching at a Christian college? No. There is no Christian higher education certification, no CCCU credential for individuals and no faith-integration certificate, and there is no licensure of any kind for postsecondary teaching. State teaching licensure is the K-12 path and does not apply. The credentials that exist are academic degrees, the institution's own accreditation, and whatever doctrinal agreement the institution asks a candidate to sign. What to do next Three documents and one date decide most of what is still open. The documents are the institution's published statement of faith or community covenant; the faculty handbook or catalog section on part-time appointments, which the department or the office of academic affairs can send you; and the posting, read for the degree and the graduate coursework it names in the teaching discipline. The date is the term start on that institution's academic calendar, counted back to whenever the department finalizes section staffing - so ask the chair when that decision is made and what enrollment a section has to clear to run. If your qualification is professional rather than academic, ask the same chair what the credential file needs, and assemble transcripts and evidence of professional standing before you send anything. Job Openings Computer Science Faculty Calvin University Grand Rapids, Michigan Mathematics & Statistics Faculty Calvin University Grand Rapids, Michigan Civil Engineering Faculty Northwestern College ORANGE CITY, Iowa Adjunct Faculty – College Math for Elementary Teaching Moody Bible Institute Chicago, Illinois Assistant Professor of Children and Family Ministry Moody Bible Institute Chicago, Illinois View All Jobs Find the Job Education Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 18 min read Christian College Jobs in Texas 2026 23 min read Nursing and Health Sciences Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 18 min read Find Jobs By Category Education Faculty Careers - Health Professions Faculty Careers - Biology/Life Sciences Faculty Careers - Psychology Faculty Careers - Engineering Faculty Careers - Computer science Faculty Careers - Communication/Design/Media Faculty Careers - Religion Powered by Web Scribble Solutions , Inc. About Our Work & Mission Our Institutions Leadership Contact Us Join Us Advertise Donate Employment Membership Sponsorship Follow Us Council for Christian Colleges & Universities 20 M Street SE, Suite 350 , Washington, D.C. 20003 202.546.8713"
    },
    "https://careers.cccu.org/career-resources/find-the-job-1/education-faculty-jobs-at-christian-colleges-roles-pay-and-day-to-day-2026-107": {
      "title": "Education Faculty Jobs",
      "description": "Education Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 | Council for Christian Colleges and Universities Home Search Directory Contact Us Career Resources Career Advice Interview Coach Offer Analyzer Pricing Register Job Seekers Employers Sign In Post a Job Career Advice Find the Job Education Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 Education Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 An education faculty line at a small Christian college is usually a small department's job: a handful of colleagues who teach the foundations and methods courses, advise candidates through the program, supervise them on placement, and carry the assessment paperwork. The Bureau of Labor Statistics put the annual median wage for Education Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1081) at $75,350 in May 2025, alongside an annual mean of $79,350 across the 60,830 jobs the same survey counted nationally. The code covers college-level teaching in education at every institution type, and nothing in the federal statistical system separates out the faith-based ones — the first thing to know before reading any pay figure. For Employers Hire faculty and staff for your campus Post your opening where faculty, student-life and campus-ministry professionals search first. Post a Job → Job Seekers Find your next role at a Christian college Browse faculty, administration and student-life jobs at faith-based colleges and universities. Browse Jobs → Key takeaway: No federal occupation, wage series or projection isolates faculty at a Christian college. The anchor for an education-teaching line is Education Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1081), at a national annual median of $75,350 in May 2025; the nearest published proxy is private colleges and universities nationally; and everything specific to a faith-based employer comes from published documents that differ campus to campus. The federal occupation this job sits inside What SOC 25-1081 covers Faculty who teach education, counseling, curriculum and teacher preparation at the college level are counted under SOC 25-1081, Education Teachers, Postsecondary. It is one of the codes BLS breaks out by field, which makes it more informative than the alternative: faculty in fields BLS does not publish separately fall into 25-1199, Postsecondary Teachers, All Other, defined as all postsecondary teachers not listed separately. If your field is one BLS does not name, the residual code carries most Christian-college faculty lines . Two cautions travel with it. A doctoral-granting private university, a state campus and a 1,200-student residential college all report into the same cell. And BLS publishes no hourly wage for it — the hourly mean and every percentile carry footnote 4 in the May 2025 national file — so any hourly figure was produced by dividing, not by the Bureau. Why no figure here says Christian college There is no SOC occupation, no O*NET occupation, no wage series and no projection for faculty at faith-based institutions. The closest approximation is the industry cut for NAICS 611305, Colleges, Universities, and Professional Schools, Privately Owned — every private college in the country, secular and sectarian. Call it private colleges and universities nationally. That industry cut exists nationally only. The Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics FAQ answers the question directly: No. BLS publishes estimates by industry and public/private ownership type at the national level only. So the sentence a job-seeker most wants — what Christian college education professors earn in a given state — cannot be built from federal data at all. Four vintages are live at once Wages, means and percentiles here are OEWS May 2025. Growth and openings come from the Employment Projections matrix on a 2024 base year, which is economy-wide and includes the self-employed, so it never agrees with an OEWS establishment count. Occupational Outlook Handbook profiles still display May 2024 medians, and O*NET descriptor data is database 30.3, released May 2026, which is not a BLS statistic. Every number here carries its own label. What the pay figures say, and what they cannot The national spread For Education Teachers, Postsecondary (SOC 25-1081), OEWS May 2025 records an annual median of $75,350, an annual mean of $79,350, a 10th percentile of $39,530 and a 90th percentile of $126,860, across 60,830 jobs nationally. The ends matter: a visiting line at a small college and an endowed chair in a graduate school of education are the same code, not the same salary. The private-college cut, where BLS publishes one BLS publishes a private-college industry cut for several codes around this one, and in every code where both figures exist the private-college number is the lower one: Occupation (OEWS May 2025) All industries, annual median Private colleges nationally, annual median Postsecondary Teachers, All Other (25-1199) $77,640 $72,400 Philosophy and Religion Teachers, Postsecondary (25-1126) $80,260 $78,660 Education Administrators, Postsecondary (11-9033) $104,590 $102,790 Educational, Guidance, and Career Counselors and Advisors (21-1012) $64,330 $58,710 No row is an education-faculty figure and none is a Christian-college figure; they show the market a private institution hires in, so read the 25-1081 median with that tilt, not as a floor. What these tables support is set out in the guide to reading federal wage data for this sector . States, and the point where the code changes No state estimate for education faculty appears here, and that is deliberate: a state figure pools public and private institutions, because the ownership split publishes nationally only, and federal wage estimates are not adjusted for cost of living. Where BLS suppresses a cell it marks the estimate as not released rather than filling it. A department chair who still teaches is a faculty line with a course release; a program director, associate dean or dean is usually reported under SOC 11-9033, Education Administrators, Postsecondary, whose OEWS May 2025 annual median is $104,590 across all industries and $102,790 inside private colleges nationally. That is a different occupation, not the top of a faculty scale, and a ladder built from both medians misleads unless it says the code changed. The move is covered in the route from a teaching line into academic administration . The work across an academic year Before the term Summer is not empty in a teacher-preparation department. Course preparation runs alongside program admissions, which at a small college the faculty often run themselves: applicant files, prerequisite coursework, interviews. Placement scheduling starts here too — relationship work with partner sites that nobody else can do for you. Advising loads are assigned in the same weeks, and where a required core exists those conversations cover the whole degree. In term: the schedule, the reading and the driving The visible load is the teaching schedule. The invisible one is the reading behind it — lesson plans, philosophy-of-education papers, observation write-ups and portfolio submissions, all long-form and none of it quick to grade. Committee service is not optional either: a department of three carries the same governance surface as a department of thirty. The part applicants most often underestimate is the travel. Supervising candidates on placement means being on site on a calendar that belongs to somebody else, in a radius set by wherever the partner sites are — on top of a teaching schedule, not instead of it. Ask how supervision is loaded, how mileage is handled, and whether it counts toward the teaching load. Assessment, accreditation layers, and the rules that govern a teaching load Education departments carry more program-level assessment than most, and at a small institution the faculty member collects the data and writes the report. Federal regulation keeps the layers distinct: 34 CFR 602.3 defines an institutional accrediting agency as one that accredits institutions of higher education, and a programmatic accrediting agency as one that accredits specific educational programs. Neither substitutes for the other, so read the department's pages and the posting for which bodies a program answers to. Faculty also sit outside the salary machinery that governs most staff jobs. Under 29 CFR 541.303, the requirements of 541.300 and Subpart G (salary requirements) of this part do not apply to the teaching professionals described in this section — an employee with a primary duty of teaching, tutoring, instructing or lecturing as a teacher in an educational establishment, which 29 CFR 541.204(b) confirms an institution of higher education is. So a teaching line is exempt on duties, with no minimum salary test, and that does not travel to non-teaching colleagues, who are tested under the administrative or professional rules and the salary threshold. The mission dimension, from published documents The hiring standard and how far it reaches The Council for Christian Colleges & Universities (CCCU), founded in 1976, is a membership association — not an accreditor, and not a certifying body. Its membership rules include an employment standard. The Updated 2024-2025 CCCU Membership Application states it as core standard 5: CCCU voting members have a continuing institutional policy and practice, effective throughout membership, to hire as full-time faculty members and administrators only persons who profess faith in Jesus Christ. The association's membership page, read on 2026-08-09, states the same requirement for governing and associate members. The scope is the point. It reaches full-time faculty members and administrators, and binds governing and associate members; it does not extend to part-time or support roles, and collaborative partners are not held to it. A full-time education faculty line at a governing or associate member is inside it; part-time teaching appointments sit outside that particular standard , though an institution's own policies may say something different, which is why its documents matter more than the association's. Three different documents, and 37 different denominations A statement of faith, a doctrinal agreement and a lifestyle or community covenant are three different documents with different footing, and merging them into one phrase is how candidates get surprised late in a search. Expect them as a routine, published part of the application at governing and associate member institutions, and expect them to differ. The association's live member directory, retrieved 2026-08-09, listed 177 institutions — 117 governing members, 19 associate members, 4 collaborative partners and 37 international affiliates — of which 131 carry a U.S. state address across 32 states, reporting 37 distinct denominational affiliations: most often Nondenominational, at 16 institutions, then Baptist at 13, Inter-denominational at 10, and Southern Baptist and Christian Churches and Churches of Christ at 8 each. Membership is held by an institution — there are five categories, of which only Governing Members vote — so there is nothing here an individual can earn. The same application's sixth core standard is Christian Distinctions and Advocacy. Under it, governing and associate members voluntarily agree to align as institutions with the association's advocacy positions, which its Board of Directors decides and which the document says change over time; those positions include a stated position on marriage and sexual ethics. It is a published criterion of a private association, noted because it is material to anyone deciding whether to apply. The legal frame, quoted and left there Title VII provides that it shall not apply to a religious corporation, association, educational institution, or society with respect to the employment of individuals of a particular religion to perform work connected with the carrying on by such corporation, association, educational institution, or society of its activities (42 U.S.C. 2000e-1(a)). It also provides that it is not an unlawful employment practice for a school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning to hire and employ employees of a particular religion if such school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning is, in whole or in substantial part, owned, supported, controlled, or managed by a particular religion or by a particular religious corporation, association, or society, or if the curriculum of such school, college, university, or other educational institution or institution of learning is directed toward the propagation of a particular religion (42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(e)(2)). Both are limited to individuals of a particular religion. The enforcing agency states the limit in its own guidance: religious organizations are subject to the Title VII prohibitions against discrimination on the basis of race, color, sex, national origin (as well as the anti-discrimination provisions of the ADEA, ADA, and GINA), and related retaliation, but are permitted to assert the statutory exemption as an affirmative defense. That guidance is the EEOC Compliance Manual, which says on its face that its contents do not have the force and effect of law and are not meant to bind the public in any way. Whether a particular employer is a religious organization for these purposes is fact-specific. On the education-program side, Title IX provides that this section shall not apply to an educational institution which is controlled by a religious organization if the application of this subsection would not be consistent with the religious tenets of such organization (20 U.S.C. 1681(a)(3)), and 34 CFR 106.12 states that this part does not apply to an educational institution which is controlled by a religious organization to the extent application of this part would not be consistent with the religious tenets of such organization , adding that an institution is not required to seek assurance from the Assistant Secretary in order to assert such an exemption. Absence from a published list therefore proves nothing. The Supreme Court's ministerial-exception cases — Hosanna-Tabor Evangelical Lutheran Church and School v. EEOC, 565 U.S. 171 (2012), and Our Lady of Guadalupe School v. Morrissey-Berru, 591 U.S. ___ (2020), Nos. 19-267 and 19-348, decided July 8 2020 — both arose from schools below the college level, and whether that doctrine reaches faculty at a Christian college is contested and fact-specific. If your question is what an employer may ask or act on, read the primary texts above, contact the EEOC, and take advice on your own facts; this is general information and not legal advice. Degrees, accreditation and the credentials that do not exist The degree the posting asks for A doctorate — an EdD or a PhD — is the usual ask for a continuing faculty line, and the requirement comes from the institution and, where relevant, the accreditor involved, not from federal rule. Read the posting for the wording: whether the doctorate is required at appointment or may be completed within a stated period, and whether prior practitioner experience is required or preferred. Accreditor expectations are frequently misread as national rules. The Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) states in its own instructions that the guidelines represent commonly-accepted good practice for the academic qualifications of faculty; however, SACSCOC recognizes that qualifications other than academic credentials (or combined with credentials) may be appropriate for teaching particular courses. That is guidance, it binds only institutions SACSCOC accredits, and the standard itself asks the institution to justify and document the qualifications of its faculty members — which is why a strong file explains the fit between your preparation and the courses named. There is no certification for this sector No Christian higher education certification exists: no association certification for faculty, no faith-integration certificate, no Christian-college teaching credential, no licensure of any kind for postsecondary teaching. A state teaching license is the route into school classrooms; it does not apply to postsecondary faculty, and no state licenses them. Where prior licensed practice matters to a department, it appears in the posting as experience the institution wants, not as a requirement of law. Who accredits what Accreditation belongs to the employer, never to the candidate. Federal regulation recognizes only institutional and programmatic accrediting agencies; there is no regulatory category of regional accreditor, that distinction having been eliminated by the Department of Education's 2019 rule at 84 FR 58834, effective 1 July 2020. Write institutional accreditor recognized by the U.S. Department of Education. The CCCU requires U.S. members to be in good standing with one of eight bodies: the Accrediting Commission for Community and Junior Colleges (ACCJC), the Higher Learning Commission (HLC), the Middle States Commission on Higher Education (MSCHE), the New England Commission of Higher Education (NECHE), the Northwest Commission on Colleges and Universities (NWCCU), the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC), the WASC Senior College & University Commission (WSCUC), and the Transnational Association of Christian Colleges and Schools (TRACS), the last carrying the association's footnote that the institution must be recognized as a four-year liberal arts institution. TRACS stands on the same footing as the others: the 2019 rule records that the Department applies the same standards for recognition to both national and regional accrediting agencies. ACCJC's federal scope covers colleges whose primary mission is granting associate degrees. Two further bodies come up in this sector and neither is on that list: the ATS Commission on Accrediting, related to but separate from The Association of Theological Schools, accredits graduate schools of theology; the Association for Biblical Higher Education's Commission on Accreditation accredits Bible colleges. Federal law protects mission inside accreditation review: 20 U.S.C. 1099b(a)(4)(A) requires a recognized accrediting agency to consistently apply and enforce standards that respect the stated mission of the institution of higher education, including religious missions , and 34 CFR 600.2 defines a religious mission as a published institutional mission that is approved by the governing body of an institution of postsecondary education and that includes, refers to, or is predicated upon religious tenets, beliefs, or teachings. Note what that requires: published, and approved by the governing body — a document you can read before you apply. How the job differs by institution type The small residential college At one or two thousand students the department is small and the faculty member is a generalist: multiple preparations, program administration, advising, admissions and placement supervision in one appointment. The search is run by a committee of colleagues, so your file is read by the people you would teach beside. Membership rules shape the curriculum too: a member campus must require general education at a minimum of 25 percent of the credits for a baccalaureate degree, including at least six credits in Biblical studies, Christian worldview or Christian ethics, of which at least three must be in Biblical studies — which is why Biblical studies teaching lines exist on campuses with no seminary attached . And education majors count only in the other-majors category toward the fifteen majors a member campus must offer, no more than three of them. The larger private university A doctoral-granting private university runs graduate cohorts, often evening or hybrid, and frequently an EdD. That changes the week: evening teaching, dissertation or capstone supervision, and a scholarship expectation stated in the appointment letter. Department structure is deeper, so a new colleague rarely carries program administration in year one. Anyone arriving from the public sector should read what actually changes in that move : most of the differences are documentary rather than academic. The seminary or Bible college A seminary or Bible college is a different employer with a different accreditation layer, and in the association's scheme those institutions typically hold associate rather than governing membership — a category still bound by the employment standard. Where a graduate theological school is involved, the ATS Commission's Standard 8.3 describes faculty qualifications as typically demonstrated through each faculty member having an appropriate doctorate and relevant professional, ecclesial or denominational experience, while allowing documented exceptions. If your teaching would touch health or clinical programs, the frame changes again — nursing and health-sciences faculty sit under their own codes and figures . Where to start The documents, in order Start with the institution's published mission statement, which federal regulation and the association's rules both require to be published and board-approved. Then the statement of faith, the doctrinal agreement and the community or lifestyle covenant, as separate documents, on the institution's own site. Then the department's pages, for the programs you would teach into and the bodies they answer to. Then the posting: the degree required at appointment, whether placement supervision is inside the teaching load or beside it, the rank, and the start date, which tells you which academic year the line belongs to. The people and the sources For application mechanics, the human-resources office; for the work itself, the search-committee chair named in the posting. Ask the chair how supervision is loaded, how program assessment is distributed, and how the required core interacts with the education sequence. For legal questions, the primary texts above and the EEOC; for wage figures, the OEWS tables at bls.gov/news.release/ocwage.t01.htm and bls.gov/oes/tables.htm. If no full-time line is open where you are, the documented route from part-time teaching into a continuing appointment is the one most people in this sector actually take. CCCU Career Center Hiring or job-hunting in Christian higher education? Post a Job → Browse Jobs → Frequently Asked Questions Which federal occupation covers education faculty at a Christian college? College-level teaching in education is reported under SOC 25-1081, Education Teachers, Postsecondary. No federal occupation isolates faculty at a faith-based institution, so 25-1081 pools every institution type together. The nearest published approximation for the sector is the industry cut for privately owned colleges and universities nationally, which BLS publishes at the national level only. What does an education faculty job at a private college pay? BLS put the annual median for Education Teachers, Postsecondary at $75,350 in May 2025, with an annual mean of $79,350 and a range from $39,530 at the 10th percentile to $126,860 at the 90th, across 60,830 jobs nationally. There is no faith-based cut of that figure. Does the association hiring standard apply to every job on a member campus? No. As published, the standard reaches full-time faculty members and administrators, and binds governing and associate members. It does not extend to part-time or support roles, and collaborative partners are not held to it. Institutions set their own policies, so the institution's published documents decide what applies to a specific post. Do I need a state teaching license to teach education courses at a college? No. State teaching licensure is the route into school classrooms and does not apply to postsecondary faculty. A doctorate, usually an EdD or a PhD, is the typical ask for a continuing college faculty line, and the requirement comes from the institution and its accreditor rather than from federal rule. Is there a certification in Christian higher education worth holding? There is none to hold. No Christian higher education certification, faith-integration certificate or Christian-college teaching credential exists, and association membership is held by an institution rather than by a person. What is real is the academic degree, the employer's accreditation, and whatever doctrinal agreement the institution requires. Job Openings Computer Science Faculty Calvin University Grand Rapids, Michigan Mathematics & Statistics Faculty Calvin University Grand Rapids, Michigan Civil Engineering Faculty Northwestern College ORANGE CITY, Iowa Adjunct Faculty – College Math for Elementary Teaching Moody Bible Institute Chicago, Illinois Assistant Professor of Children and Family Ministry Moody Bible Institute Chicago, Illinois View All Jobs Find the Job Adjunct Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 18 min read Christian College Jobs in Texas 2026 23 min read Nursing and Health Sciences Faculty Jobs at Christian Colleges: Roles Pay and Day-to-Day 2026 18 min read Find Jobs By Category Education Faculty Careers - Health Professions Faculty Careers - Biology/Life Sciences Faculty Careers - Psychology Faculty Careers - Engineering Faculty Careers - Computer science Faculty Careers - Communication/Design/Media Faculty Careers - Religion Powered by Web Scribble Solutions , Inc. About Our Work & Mission Our Institutions Leadership Contact Us Join Us Advertise Donate Employment Membership Sponsorship Follow Us Council for Christian Colleges & Universities 20 M Street SE, Suite 350 , Washington, D.C. 20003 202.546.8713"
